Everything needed to reproduce the simulations of CTC and CTC clusters in a microfluidic channel
at four different shear stress levels (including 0), calibrated with experimental data. 

This set reproduces all the results reported in Section 3.1 Calibration results in the 
following preprint:




To run all conditions, simply run run_simulations.py. For instance as 

python3 run_simulations.py

in our case. 

Then use the following scripts for post-processing:

cluster_maps.py - visualizations of cells and clusters inside the channel at select times

get_statistics.py - statistics on area ratios, cluster numbers, and sizes. Statistics are averages and standard deviations over averages across each realizations. Specifically, time averages across a predefined time window in every individual realization. Cluster sizes are calculated only from existing clusters.

failure_statistics.py - %bonds that failed in tension, shear, and alongation. Average over all realizations.

single_realization.py - time evolution of the above three properties for one realization that is closest to the ensemble average w.r.t. its area ratio; it indicates the averaging interval.

time_evolution.py - time evolution of the three properties (ratios, numbers, sizes) for each realization and their instantenous average across all realizations.

Other files (that do not need modification)

simulation.cpp - core C++ code for setting up and running a simulation (and all realizations)

make_initial.py - creates initial configuration i.e. spatial distribution of single cells and clusters of various sizes

compilation.py - compiles the C++ code

parameters.txt - all input parameters (with changes set by the run_simulations.py driver)

experimental_area.txt - area ratios (in %) for each wall shear stress scenario (0, 2, 5, and 20) from 

Marrella A, Fedi A, Varani G, et al (2021) 
High blood flow shear stress values are associated with circulating tumor cells cluster disaggregation in a multi-channel microfluidic device.
PLoS One 16(1):e0245536. 
https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0245536   
